		{"id":"99ef3e8d13365401","type":"text","text":"Water and liquids","x":-17666,"y":4434,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1ffb7f0f823336df","type":"text","text":"Dust: secondary animation","x":-17657,"y":3773,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0cfeb2df217ce89e","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Usage/Forza5Dust.jpg","x":-17292,"y":3520,"width":1126,"height":626},
		{"id":"9d94a171e256aa13","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Usage/BlackFlagRain.jpg","x":-17292,"y":2883,"width":1110,"height":491},
		{"id":"e24f42bb20d27541","type":"text","text":"Create Particle Network (e.g., POP Network):\n* Type of dynamics network: besides particles (POP) we have RBD, fluid, etc. simulation.","x":-14060,"y":11270,"width":486,"height":113},
		{"id":"7503ffbbb422a1ed","type":"text","text":"POP Object:\n* Simulation object: currently, we are simulating particles, thus we have POP object\n* It defines what will be output of the network: simulated particles\n* Contains information from the simulation\n* It feeds next frame of simulation","x":-14360,"y":11508,"width":320,"height":362},
		{"id":"cd8333e8b0fa9434","type":"text","text":"Solver:\n* Brain of simulation network\n* Calculates per frame data: currently, we are simulating particles","x":-13942,"y":11521,"width":250,"height":249},
		{"id":"54eacac3048b185a","type":"text","text":"Sources:\n* Incoming (source) data to simulation: data/geometry brought into simulation\n* Adds data to simulation","x":-13420,"y":11507,"width":340,"height":263},
		{"id":"abdc7c6401c1ca7a","type":"text","text":"Here, after source and before Solver we add collisions, forces, etc. - behavour is added to source","x":-13402,"y":11849,"width":305,"height":177},
		{"id":"f3e167c493705e0f","type":"text","text":"After solver we can add forces","x":-13947,"y":11873,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"21e0db564bc0778a","type":"text","text":"Particle system \"solving\" in each iteration in Houdini is implemented in `POP Solver Node` \n\nPOP Solver Node can be used in:\n* /obj/dop/ - Root -> Object (OBJ) Network -> Dynamics (DOP) Network\n* /obj/geo/ - Root -> Object (OBJ) Network -> Geometry (SOP) network\n\t* In newer versions of Houdini, DOP network is being added inside SOP network for easier workflow. However, creating simulations in DOP if more flexible and powerful\n\t* Therefore, When POP Network Node is created in /obj/geo/ it is called DOP network and inside Houdini creates base POP network nodes: POP solver and other (e.g., POP object and POP source)\n\nNB: we can create multiple POP Network nodes in SOP.","x":-12508,"y":11577,"width":742,"height":360},
		{"id":"48d8d45625471e8a","type":"text","text":"Movement of particles is defined by their velocity and time. For example, if velocity is (3,1,0) and particle starting from (0,0,0) then in 1second it will be at position (3,1,0).\n\nSecond in Houdini is 24 frames. Frames in Houdini can be integer of float (sub frames).\n\nWe don't usually modify velocity of particles directly, we apply forces which are then solved into resulting force vector, which are then solved into acceleration and velocity. \n\nForce can be activated, deactivated or changed any time and keyframed.\n\nNote that force is also an attribute which each particles stores alongside position and velocity. We can track each attribute for each point for each frame using \"Spreadsheet\". \n\nAlso, we can visualize each attribute in Scene view (Guide).\n\nMultiple forces can be added to our particle system. Force is driving particle system and we can use various presets or create our own using VOPs and VEX.","x":-12508,"y":11989,"width":742,"height":440},
		{"id":"ddfb27ba17127376","type":"text","text":"POP Wrangle","x":-9768,"y":11248,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b01e2acf05fdd5c8","type":"text","text":"POP Collision","x":-9768,"y":11370,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"538986e2c570b491","type":"text","text":"POP Solver","x":-10288,"y":11666,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"18f87f1d175e8dcc","type":"text","text":"POP source\n* Place where we define how particles are inputted to particle system and initial attributes","x":-10973,"y":11666,"width":250,"height":220},
		{"id":"f7ad860a075f34ce","type":"text","text":"POP Object","x":-10645,"y":11670,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4d445f4811561ae5","type":"text","text":"Attributes\n* **Inherit Attributes**: all attributes that can be inherited from the SOP/DOP input, e.g., color (Cd), velocity (v)\n\t* Using inherited velocity of moving geometry which has computed velocity attribute give nice results - form of particle movement is not random but relies on underlaying geometry movement\n* **Initial Velocity**: velocity (v) attrib that is created when particle is created\n\t* Use Inherit Velocity\n\t* Add to Inherit Velocity\n\t* Add Initial Velocity","x":-9945,"y":12186,"width":891,"height":490},
		{"id":"6813d73807260346","type":"text","text":"Source\n* **Emission type** - how to scatter points on input (surfaces, points, etc.)\n* **Geometry source** - which input (1,2,3,4, SOP Path etc.) we use for scattering points. Using SOP/DOP path we don't need to connect anything to POPNetwork node directly.\n* **Group** - which polygons/points will be used for emission\n* **Emission Attribute**: emit only from points which have this attribute - this can be used as mask","x":-11468,"y":12176,"width":623,"height":500},
		{"id":"197092c8dc512c25","type":"text","text":"Birth\n* **Impulse Activation** - spawn particles impulse mode. 0 is off, any other value is on  (on or off -> 1 or 0)\n\t* **Impulse count** - spawn N particles per frame. After F frames we will have N * F particles\n* **Const Activation** - spawn particles const mode. 0 is off, any other value is on  (on or off -> 1 or 0)\n\t* **Const. Birth rate** - spawn N particles per second (24 frames)\n* **Seed** - seed for jittering spawn position\n* **Life expectancy** - how many seconds particles will live. If we set 0.5 they will live 12 frames. It sets up **life** **attribute** (constant over frames)\n\t* **Life variance** - new life expectancy = life expectancy +/- that value\n\t* **age attribute** gives information for each frame how long the particle has been alive (changes over frames). Once age is 0, particle is destroyed\n\t* Creating **dead attribute** kills particle, e.g., using **POP wrangle node**.","x":-10738,"y":12182,"width":620,"height":494},
		{"id":"17d4e945921ebf11","type":"text","text":"Lines confirming shape","x":-5420,"y":11442,"width":300,"height":68},
		{"id":"e7570465c3c62a4d","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Envs/POPSourceAttributes.jpg","x":-9945,"y":12756,"width":891,"height":465},
		{"id":"fd39bbaf56624ca1","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Envs/POPSourceSource.jpg","x":-11465,"y":12756,"width":620,"height":465},
		{"id":"48fccee2c800d7f8","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Envs/POPSourceBirth.jpg","x":-10738,"y":12756,"width":620,"height":465},
		{"id":"344779a71bd1092d","type":"text","text":"TODO: flipbooks","x":-5375,"y":12303,"width":505,"height":160,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"29eff8c23cd5956a","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/SDFTrails.jpg","x":-4800,"y":11287,"width":647,"height":446},
		{"id":"d0e8945cc8fa0c99","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/1_Mesh.jpg","x":-4060,"y":11287,"width":420,"height":449},
		{"id":"9d124ed0d6ded79c","type":"text","text":"0. Pick Mesh (e.g., https://polyhaven.com/models)","x":-4060,"y":11733,"width":420,"height":90},
		{"id":"e92041a4d7861922","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/2_SDFBakeFromMesh.jpg","x":-3561,"y":11287,"width":409,"height":446},
		{"id":"f4719ad63dc0fc5f","type":"text","text":"1. Bake SDF from Mesh","x":-3556,"y":11724,"width":404,"height":54},
		{"id":"b53a433b8b51e45d","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/3_BaseVFXGraph.jpg","x":-3071,"y":11287,"width":525,"height":437},
		{"id":"b23afef02438742a","type":"text","text":"2. Create base VFX Graph","x":-3071,"y":11724,"width":525,"height":58},
		{"id":"8316a85248afc115","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/4_InitializeSDFPosition.jpg","x":-2440,"y":11283,"width":468,"height":445},
		{"id":"7ac0b867257a56ff","type":"text","text":"3. Initialize: set position using SDF. Also:\n\t* Output: Change particle texture and size\n\t* Spawn&Initialize: Increase rate and capacity\n\t* Configure lifetime\n\nNOTE: If Particles are flickering, click on Update Node and in inspector turn off sorting: https://discussions.unity.com/t/vfx-graph-particles-flickering-in-alpha-blend-mode/235655","x":-2440,"y":11723,"width":468,"height":280},
		{"id":"46c0e5afa77a9ff9","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/1/5_UpdateOutputTurbulenceSDFConformColor.jpg","x":-1900,"y":11287,"width":413,"height":459},
		{"id":"9cb7a01f0cd79a9f","type":"text","text":"4. Update: add turbulence force and SDF conform force. Also add in Output: by Speed or location","x":-1900,"y":11746,"width":413,"height":116},
		{"id":"789d0e7e964f3da3","type":"text","text":"Constraints","x":-10190,"y":1143,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e3f43f26075f0cd3","type":"text","text":"Behaviour in time","x":-10759,"y":980,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"70387f61d2f832f6","type":"text","text":"Examples of implicit functions:\n* 3D noise functions","x":-11320,"y":1593,"width":250,"height":168},
		{"id":"4730130918f15a29","type":"text","text":"This way, vector field can be computed from mesh or curve (e.g., normals and tangents) and stored in voxel grid.","x":-11020,"y":1593,"width":261,"height":168},
		{"id":"98113e2a143659f5","type":"text","text":"Implicit: function evaluated for each element on demand","x":-11320,"y":1457,"width":250,"height":110},
		{"id":"34d9c634f12e6253","type":"text","text":"Explicit: vector field is stored in voxel grid and used for nearest element.","x":-11015,"y":1457,"width":250,"height":110},
		{"id":"e5c1a62b5a935d37","type":"text","text":"Algorithm driving particles","x":-11160,"y":1143,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"20ee76cf1075ee6c","type":"text","text":"Vector field attribute","x":-11160,"y":1203,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e3e80cffd319e53d","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Concepts/VectorField.png","x":-10740,"y":1531,"width":256,"height":230},
		{"id":"00b576d3e29ac693","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Concepts/VectorField2D.png","x":-10740,"y":1260,"width":255,"height":251},
		{"id":"feceee83104e1dbb","type":"text","text":"Usually mesh inside particle system domain interfering with particle movement.","x":-10190,"y":1310,"width":250,"height":106},
		{"id":"ab03d24e92c587d2","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/Concepts/ParicleCollisions.png","x":-10382,"y":1453,"width":442,"height":247},
		{"id":"658976f3f5c085fe","type":"text","text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cm2juuIXhM4","x":-10382,"y":1701,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"494930ac76d40c49","type":"text","text":"Fuzzy models via physical simulation","x":-13180,"y":4615,"width":300,"height":75,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a123a317eb34085f","type":"text","text":"Point cloud open and filter","x":-3793,"y":3467,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"90c8a934e7eb7822","type":"text","text":"particle system advect by volumes - feedback loop:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/dop/popadvectbyvolumes.html","x":-3383,"y":3508,"width":320,"height":168},
		{"id":"c70cca31a8854343","type":"text","text":"Particle system","x":-3348,"y":3388,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9737fbea700a8ddc","type":"text","text":"Useful for:\n* Custom vector fields\n* Phenomenological fluid simulation\n\t* https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/sop/volumerasterize.html","x":-2057,"y":3677,"width":380,"height":204},
		{"id":"e105bd1efe59fb2c","type":"text","text":"Particle-based fluid simulation approaches:\n* SPH \n* PSD\n* FLIP\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bx_D_51bF7Y","x":-2103,"y":4074,"width":724,"height":200},
		{"id":"46f0fbcf35151d85","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/VolumesParticles2.jpg","x":-2557,"y":3707,"width":400,"height":247},
		{"id":"5f3cb7798841531e","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/VolumeRasterizeAttributes.jpg","x":-2503,"y":4054,"width":400,"height":239},
		{"id":"76ad8926facb7e7f","type":"text","text":"Base geometrical shape:\n* Spheres\n* Lines\n* etc.","x":-5008,"y":3343,"width":250,"height":155},
		{"id":"9a640c701611862d","type":"text","text":"Example 3 (file: SurfaceTurbulence):\n* Create sourcing geometry and initial attribs (Cd, N, v, etc.)\n\t* IMPORTANT: attribute v: sourcing geometry was used to compute vector field (v) which is further used for driving particles inside POP Net using POP VOP\n* Use POP Net for particle system\n* Use Point VOP to drive particles using vector field computed from sourcing geometry\n* Use POP Force with noise to make flow turbulent\n*  After POP Net, use particles to form trails","x":-5875,"y":1963,"width":807,"height":277},
		{"id":"a0efd0cca5a796d1","type":"text","text":"Assigning geometry vectors to volume vectors","x":-3794,"y":3616,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"709903a13b61cd40","type":"text","text":"Empty Volume","x":-3793,"y":3330,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"799d0d1ba62b3877","type":"text","text":"Adding additional attributes to points of geometry:\n* Color","x":-5008,"y":3547,"width":250,"height":129},
		{"id":"b8f97c47a924102d","type":"text","text":"Geometry normals","x":-4348,"y":3437,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3ea9390e8fe96b80","type":"text","text":"Tangents on surface geometry","x":-4338,"y":3587,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d30fa5afbe2bccde","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/5/SurfaceVectorField1.png","x":-5012,"y":1963,"width":570,"height":400},
		{"id":"3a5dd3a28d801052","type":"link","url":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/ParticleSystems.html","x":-475,"y":2311,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"4c06454e853e87ea","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/4/CurveGuideCollision.jpg","x":-5099,"y":2660,"width":675,"height":420},
		{"id":"918bd9823e289628","type":"text","text":"Example 5 (file: ParticlesVolumeAdvect):\n* Create sourcing geometry, convert to volume and compute initial attribs \n\t* NOTE:  Particles will be spawned inside volume\n* Create curve and from it tube geometry, compute vector field using this geometry, store vector field in BB volume of this geometry\n* Use POP Net for particle system\n* Inside POP Net, use POP Advect By Volumes to drive particles using vector field computed  vector stored in volume\n* After POP Net, use particles to form trails\n* Optionally, convert particles from POP Net to volume and mesh","x":-5900,"y":3826,"width":757,"height":320},
		{"id":"97877b403da123ca","type":"text","text":"Volume as datas tructure for storing vector field for particles: rasterize volume of surface containing vector field and volume advect in POP for particle simulation\n\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wR0SDptfygg\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yqM_3goH4J8\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rn_8iN7lyjI\n","x":-4203,"y":4153,"width":700,"height":280},
		{"id":"710fa593121a4305","type":"text","text":"Point Cloud Open and Point Cloud Filter VOP/VEX nodes/functions are used to access point information around current particle in POP VOP. This way, vector field stored at points can be used to drive particles.\n\nIt can be used for any kind of points in surface or volume:\n1. Surface points: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zJls__AJlJo\n2. Volume points: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JS3rw7W1gN4&t=11s ","x":-3348,"y":4157,"width":724,"height":314},
		{"id":"6d52b6b5da9319e8","type":"text","text":"Advection:\n\n* https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advection\n* In the fields of [physics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physics \"Physics\"), [engineering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engineering \"Engineering\"), and [earth sciences](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_science \"Earth science\"), **advection** is the [transport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport \"Transport\") of a substance or quantity by bulk motion of a fluid.\n* During advection, a fluid transports some conserved quantity or material via bulk motion. The fluid's motion is described [mathematically](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematics \"Mathematics\") as a [vector field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_field \"Vector field\"), and the transported material is described by a [scalar field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scalar_field \"Scalar field\") showing its distribution over space.","x":-5067,"y":4174,"width":710,"height":280},
		{"id":"6ad892c4dcb731fa","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/VolumesParticles1.jpg","x":-3413,"y":3879,"width":400,"height":230},
		{"id":"6b9a99feb7619926","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/VolumeVectorFieldFromTangents.jpg","x":-3903,"y":3869,"width":400,"height":251},
		{"id":"fe39c43c86829a69","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/InitialGeometryTangents.jpg","x":-4403,"y":3861,"width":400,"height":250},
		{"id":"2521614d04388988","type":"file","file":"Images/Fuzzy/LectureExamples/6/InitialGeometry.jpg","x":-5008,"y":3869,"width":400,"height":233},
		{"id":"47c87a69adad360a","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QpLvBIFyhuE&t=4s","x":272,"y":2851,"width":658,"height":360},
		{"id":"6621050035d8d1f8","type":"link","url":"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.visualeffectgraph@17.2/manual/index.html","x":-30,"y":2311,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"67aa783b2d1e02a9","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JfKQ8m3ZfJM","x":-520,"y":3922,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"974b4a4b3b6ae865","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FBP9k6W48vM","x":-475,"y":2851,"width":655,"height":360},
		{"id":"fecc586236fd1f76","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8OXb5GjRrTo","x":-475,"y":3281,"width":655,"height":394},
		{"id":"478be33200a0dd7b","type":"link","url":"https://unity.com/resources/creating-advanced-vfx-unity6","x":450,"y":2311,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"26f2e1542346348d","type":"text","text":"Examples:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/abstract-particles-with-vex-and-math/\n\nVelocity field:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zJls__AJlJo&t=311s\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m7GqGjqI7CU\n* https://www.reddit.com/r/Simulated/comments/1kd4yx3/exploring_layered_velocity_fields_in_particle/\n\nCollision detection:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uHzGzOHeQcs\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IECGTtgfVnU\n\nInstance and attract:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?app=desktop&v=ef-0RyQKKgo&t=0s\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=edPvHXKvOtg\n\nInstance and flow:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UWMcv6R8bmk\n\nFollow path:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/particles-along-curve-project-walkthrough/\n\nInherit geometry tangent field:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6H50Ww5JZXg\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GrJcDCb6LLo\n\nAttractor examples using SOP solver and points:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=saA6-edb-OE\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vl1tLebo-xY\n\nSurface flow:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2hkKtms_qqA\n\nForces:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaByH3yyzN4\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zOL6LLH7ZaQ","x":440,"y":917,"width":780,"height":1083},
		{"id":"b5f6d6f5bbd31c53","type":"link","url":"https://engineering.purdue.edu/~ebertd/book2e.html","x":230,"y":31,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"59c875b8e9ed0a64","type":"text","text":"Particle advection by volume vector field\n\nExample:\n1. Mesh is used to generate main shape (only surface).\n2. Differential geometry OPs are used to compute information per surface points (e.g., tangent field)\n3. Surface is rasterized into volume grid where each cell is assigned with vector value\n4. Particle simulation is using volume grid information for movement.\n\nExample:\n1. Mesh is used to generate main shape (only surface).\n2. Mesh is rasterized: converted to grid\n3. Noise is computed for each grid cell resulting in volume vector field\n4. Particle simulation is using volume grid information for movement.\n\nTutorials:\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JS3rw7W1gN4\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rC9EGQJOLyE\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WKDY5b_V5go\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K0cNvpXujmk","x":-420,"y":1340,"width":790,"height":660},
		{"id":"3cc5e31e286f9e6a","type":"text","text":"Basics:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/learn/collections/applied-houdini-particles/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/intro-to-houdini-particles/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/\n* https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1CXoX1LQ3n8\n* https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/dopparticles/attributes.html","x":-420,"y":1017,"width":640,"height":280},
		{"id":"277ce68ce80c21e4","type":"link","url":"https://natureofcode.com/particles/","x":-500,"y":31,"width":648,"height":400},
